.TH LUS 1 "" "Lus" "Lus Manual"
.SH NAME
lus \- run Lus programs and the tools of a Lus project
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBlus\fR [options] [script [args]]
\fBlus\fR <command> [arguments]
\fBlus\fR +VERSION [arguments]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBlus script.lus\fR runs a script and passes it the arguments after its name, which the script reads from \fB...\fR and from the table \fBarg\fR.
With no script and no \fB\-e\fR, \fBlus\fR starts an interactive session that runs each line as it is entered.
When the first argument is the name of a command, \fBlus\fR runs that command.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBrun\fR
Run a script.
\fBlus run script.lus\fR is the same as \fBlus script.lus\fR.
.TP
\fBtest\fR
Run the tests of a project.
.TP
\fBfmt\fR
Format source files.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
Find errors and likely mistakes without running the code.
.TP
\fBdoc\fR
Generate API documentation from doc comments.
.TP
\fBast\fR
Print the syntax tree of a file as JSON.
.TP
\fBbuild\fR
Bundle a script and its modules into a standalone executable.
.TP
\fBcompile\fR
Compile a script to a binary chunk.
.TP
\fBdebug\fR
Run a script under a debugger.
.TP
\fBhelp\fR
Print the standard library reference for a name.
.TP
\fBagents\fR
Set a project up for coding agents.
.TP
\fBtree\fR
Print the modules that a script requires, as a tree.
.TP
\fBlsp\fR
Run the language server.
.TP
\fBversion\fR
Install, list, and select releases of Lus.
.TP
\fBcompletions\fR
Print the completion script of a shell.
.SH OPTIONS
The options come before the script name, because \fBlus\fR passes everything after the script name to the script.
.TP
\fB\-e stat\fR
Run the string \fBstat\fR as a chunk, for a one-line program such as \fBlus \-e 'print(2^10)'\fR.
The option may repeat.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Enter interactive mode after \fBscript\fR ends, with its globals in place, to inspect the state it left.
.TP
\fB\-l mod\fR
Require the module \fBmod\fR and store it in the global \fBmod\fR before the script runs.
.TP
\fB\-l g=mod\fR
Require the module \fBmod\fR and store it in the global \fBg\fR.
.TP
\fB\-P perm\fR, \fB\-\-pledge perm\fR
Grant a permission to the script, such as \fB\-Pfs:read\fR or \fB\-Pnetwork\fR.
Once the command line grants a permission, the script may pledge only what the command line grants.
Use this option to limit a script that you have not read.
.TP
\fB\-F name[=value]\fR, \fB\-\-flag name[=value]\fR
Define a build flag, which the script reads from the readonly table \fBflags\fR.
A flag with no value is \fBtrue\fR.
A numeric value is a number.
Any other value is a string.
The compiler removes the branch of an \fBif flags.name\fR or \fBif not flags.name\fR that can never run with the given flags, so debugging code behind a flag costs nothing when the flag is off.
.TP
\fB\-\-include path[:alias]\fR
Compile a module file, or every module in a directory, when the interpreter starts.
\fBrequire\fR then loads the module from memory with no \fBload\fR or \fBfs:read\fR permission, so a program that has sealed its permissions can still require it.
The alias is the name of the module.
.TP
\fB\-\-readonly\-env\fR
Make the global table and the standard library tables readonly once the script starts.
An assignment to a global or to a library field then raises \fBattempt to modify a readonly table\fR, which protects a program from code that replaces a library function.
.TP
\fB\-\-sandbox\fR
Seal the permissions after the grants of \fB\-P\fR, before any code runs.
Every later \fBpledge\fR is refused, so a program run as \fBlus \-\-sandbox untrusted.lus\fR can read no file, start no process, open no connection, and load no code.
\fB\-P\fR still grants what the run needs, as in \fBlus \-\-sandbox \-P fs:read=./data script.lus\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Load remote modules only from the module cache.
A module that the cache lacks raises an error at its \fBrequire\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-reload\fR
Ask the server of every remote module whether it changed before the program runs, even when the cached copy is recent.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-prefetch\fR
Download nothing before the program runs.
Each remote module is downloaded at its \fBrequire\fR, with a warning.
.TP
\fB\-\-gc\-pause N\fR
Let the heap grow by N percent of its live size before the next collection starts.
A lower value uses less memory and more processor time.
.TP
\fB\-W\fR
Turn warnings on.
\fBwarn\fR then writes its messages to standard error.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR
Ignore the environment variables that configure the interpreter, such as \fBLUS_PATH\fR and \fBLUS_JIT\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-dis\fR
Print the bytecode of the script and exit without running it.
The script is compiled with the flags given with \fB\-F\fR.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the commands and options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-\fR
Stop handling options, for a script whose name starts with a dash.
.TP
\fB\-\fR
Stop handling options and run standard input as the script.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBLUS_PATH\fR
The initial value of \fBpackage.path\fR, the list of templates that \fBrequire\fR searches.
.TP
\fBLUS_JIT\fR
The setting of the JIT compiler.
\fBon\fR, the default, compiles the functions that run often.
\fBoff\fR interprets everything.
\fBbaseline\fR uses only the first tier.
\fBeager\fR and \fBopt\-eager\fR compile every function on its first call, which is useful for tests.
.TP
\fBLUS_JIT_THRESHOLD\fR
How many calls and loop iterations a function makes before the JIT compiles it.
Change it to measure how the threshold affects the speed of a program.
.TP
\fBLUS_HOME\fR
The directory that holds installed releases and the module cache, by default \fB~/.lus\fR, or \fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\elus\fR on Windows.
.TP
\fBLUS_RELEASE_URL\fR
The address of a mirror of the release index that \fBlus version\fR reads, in place of \fBhttps://lus.dev/releases/\fR.
.TP
\fBLUS_GC_THREADS\fR
The number of threads that the garbage collector marks with.
The default uses up to eight and leaves one processor free.
A lower value, such as \fB1\fR, leaves more processor time to other programs on the machine.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The script ended normally.
.TP
1
The script raised an error that it did not catch, or an option is malformed.
